From your screenshots, the gap is specifically between “Check In Notes” and “Action” columns.

I can already see you’re using:

.session-table .checkin-notes-col,
.session-table td.checkin-notes-col {
    width: 80px;
    padding-left: 0.1rem !important;
    padding-right: 0.1rem !important;
}
.session-table .action-col,
.session-table td.action-col {
    width: 40px;
    padding-left: 0.1rem !important;
    padding-right: 0.1rem !important;
}

The problem is usually table-layout:auto or a min-width on one of the columns.

Quick Fix #1 (Most likely)

Add:

.session-table {
    table-layout: fixed;
    width: 100%;
}

Quick Fix #2

Force both columns to shrink:

.session-table th.checkin-notes-col,
.session-table td.checkin-notes-col {
    width: 60px !important;
    max-width: 60px !important;
    min-width: 60px !important;
    padding: 0 2px !important;
}
.session-table th.action-col,
.session-table td.action-col {
    width: 40px !important;
    max-width: 40px !important;
    min-width: 40px !important;
    padding: 0 2px !important;
}

Quick Fix #3

Inspect the column before “Action”. If it contains:

white-space: nowrap;

or

display:flex;
justify-content: space-between;

remove them temporarily and check.

Quick Check in Browser

Press F12 → Inspect the empty green area → Look at the highlighted <th> width.

If the <th class="checkin-notes-col"> is showing something like 150px+, then another CSS rule is overriding your width.

Search in VS Code:

checkin-notes-col
min-width
table-layout
th

and look for any:

min-width: 120px;
flex: 1;
width: auto;

that’s overriding your new CSS.

The safest fix is usually:

.session-table {
    table-layout: fixed;
}

followed by

.checkin-notes-col { width: 60px !important; }
.action-col { width: 40px !important; }

